  "metadata": {
    "title": "Musk’s SpaceX surpasses Amazon by value in blow for Bezos",
    "byline": null,
    "published_at": null,
    "site_name": "finance.yahoo.com",
    "language": null
  },
  "content": {
    "text": "SpaceX has surpassed Amazon as the fifth-largest company in the world, delivering a major coup for Elon Musk in his battle with rival Jeff Bezos.\nThe rocket company, which joined the stock market on Friday, now has a market value of nearly $3tn (£2.2tn) after a share price surge on Tuesday – around $300bn higher than that of Amazon.\nMr Musk's company also briefly eclipsed the value of Microsoft – the world's fourth largest company – earlier in the day before paring gains.\nSpaceX's surge is likely to rekindle the rivalry between Mr Musk and Mr Bezos, who is developing his own space exploration and satellite internet ventures through Blue Origin and Amazon Leo.\nThe pair are fierce business rivals and have exchanged jibes over their vast personal fortunes.\nMr Musk joked in 2021 that he would send Mr Bezos a silver medal and a giant statue of the number two after beating him to become the world's richest person.\nMr Musk on Friday became the world's first trillionaire after floating SpaceX on the Nasdaq stock exchange. He owns a 43pc stake in the business.\nSince then, he has added a further $400bn (£300bn) to his wealth after shares jumped by almost a fifth on Monday and were up by as much as 10pc when trading began on Tuesday.\nAs a result, the value of Mr Musk's stake in the company has soared from $700bn at the time of the company's stock market debut last Friday to $1.1tn on Tuesday.\nCombined with his $290bn stake in electric carmaker Tesla and interests in other startups, Mr Musk's worth has risen to almost $1.4tn, shortly after the SpaceX float made him the world's first trillionaire.\nIt also means his net worth is now more than $1tn more than the world's second-richest person, Larry Page, the Google co-founder, who is worth $314bn.\nA rush of investor interest and a resurgence in optimism following Donald Trump's deal with Iran mean SpaceX shares have continued to soar since its record-breaking New York debut last week.\nSpaceX's continued rise means investors who acquired shares at the company's $135 flotation price have made a profit of more than 50pc in four days.\nHuge appetite led the company to raise an extra $11bn in shares on Monday, in addition to the $75bn it raised last week.\nIn a sign of further expansion plans, SpaceX on Tuesday said it would activate an option to buy the AI coding start-up Cursor in a $60bn deal.\nIt hopes buying the company, which allows software engineers to write code with the help of AI, will give the company a leg up in selling AI tools to businesses.",
